\description{
The counterpart of \code{\link[=RMitemInfit]{RMitemInfit()}} for data imputed with the \pkg{mice}
package. Computes the conditional infit MSQ in each imputed dataset and
pools the results with Rubin's rules. Items are flagged against the
simulated interval, since this function has no p-values yet (see the
section below).
}
\details{
For each of the \code{m} imputed datasets, the function:
\enumerate{
\item Fits a Rasch model by CML via \code{psychotools::pcmodel()} (a
dichotomous item is a 2-category partial credit model), consistent with
\code{\link{RMitemInfit}} and the rest of the package.
\item Computes conditional infit MSQ and its standard error via
\code{iarm::out_infit()}.
\item Computes item locations (mean of the grand-mean-centred CML
Andrich thresholds) and the mean WLE person location.
}

The per-imputation estimates are then pooled using Rubin's rules:
\describe{
\item{Pooled MSQ}{The mean of the \code{m} infit MSQ point estimates.}
\item{Within-imputation variance}{The mean of the \code{m} squared standard
errors.}
\item{Between-imputation variance}{The sample variance of the \code{m} point
estimates.}
\item{Total variance}{Within + (1 + 1/m) * Between.}
\item{Pooled SE}{The square root of the total variance.}
}

Relative item location is the mean of per-imputation relative locations
(item location minus sample mean person location).

\strong{Caveat on the pooled SE.} The within-imputation variance is the
squared conditional infit SE from \code{iarm::out_infit()}. Müller (2020)
showed that this asymptotic SE is an unreliable measure of uncertainty
for the conditional infit statistic, and Rubin's pooled SE inherits that
limitation. Read the \code{Infit_SE} column as a rough indication of the
variability between imputations, not as a standard error for
inference. For item misfit decisions, prefer the simulation-based
cutoffs from \code{\link{RMitemInfitCutoffMI}}.

Imputed datasets in which the model cannot be fitted are dropped with a
warning. So are imputed datasets in which an item lacks its lowest or a
middle response category, which can happen with \code{polr}, \code{polyreg} or
\code{norm} imputation of a rare category. The warning names the items, since
the pooled results then come only from imputations that filled the
category. If every imputation lacks the category (as with \code{pmm} when the
observed data lack it), the function stops and names the item. If all
imputations fail, the function stops with an error. At least two
imputations must succeed to estimate the between-imputation variance.

The \pkg{mice} and \pkg{iarm} packages must be installed (they are in
Suggests).
